---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import WorkedTable from '../../components/insights/WorkedTable.astro';
import PractitionerNote from '../../components/insights/PractitionerNote.astro';
import { practitionerB2 } from '../../data/practitioner-b2';
import examples from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14-operations.json';
const e = practitionerB2[2];
---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="5 min" standfirst="An extractor can return a perfectly shaped object with the wrong amount inside it, so the response, the schema and the evidence need separate checks.">
  <p>Take a document containing the amount 42.00. The schema requires a numeric amount and a currency. A response containing 24.00 passes the type check and still fails the job. No model benchmark is needed to see the gap: the schema describes the permitted shape, while the source document determines the correct value.</p>
  <p>Structured outputs are good for defining that shape. Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>documentation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes constrained JSON output, supported-schema limits and exceptions for refusals and token-limit stops. Our proposed reporting method keeps those outcomes separate from factual acceptance.</p>
  <h2>HTTP success is not task success</h2>
  <p>The documentation says a refusal can arrive with HTTP status 200 and need not match the requested schema. A response cut short at max_tokens can also be incomplete, so the completion status needs inspecting before the body is treated as an answer.</p>
  <p>Parsing and validation come next, then a check of the values against the document and the job's rules. Those stages give a better failure record than one success flag, and they show where to spend effort: a malformed result needs different attention from an accurately formatted wrong answer.</p>
  <WorkedTable headers={['Cumulative gate in a fictional run', 'Passing requests', 'Share of original run']} rows={examples.validation} caption="Invented nested counts out of 1,000 completed HTTP requests. These illustrate reporting gates, not observed provider error rates." />
  <p>In the example, 970 requests pass the schema and 900 pass the task check. Task acceptance is 90% of the original run, or about 92.8% of the schema-valid subset. Both statements are true and answer different questions, so a percentage needs its denominator beside it.</p>
  <h2>Evidence comes before the prompt</h2>
  <p>Each extracted field needs something that supports it, decided in advance. A simple document might allow a page reference and the exact text span. A calculated total may need the input lines and the calculation. A reviewer can inspect evidence; the model's confidence proves nothing.</p>
  <p>Missing information needs a representation too. If the amount is absent, an explicit missing value can be the correct result, and a schema that demands a number without allowing an absence state makes that outcome hard to express. Absent and ambiguous fields are worth testing before running a large collection.</p>
  <p>Normalisation rules need defining as well. Open points include whether a date with no year stays incomplete, whether the extractor may convert a currency symbol into a code and which decimal notation is allowed. These are application decisions. During testing, the original text should sit beside the normalised value so the conversion can be checked.</p>
  <h2>Retries depend on what failed</h2>
  <p>A refusal should stay a refusal; a blind retry loop obscures it and adds spend. For an incomplete result, check the limit and request size before trying again. For a schema-valid wrong value, more output space does not address the evidence failure, and the prompt, document handling and acceptance rule need review.</p>
  <p>Each original job gets one final result even if it has several attempts, and the spend on those attempts is counted together. Otherwise a high pass rate can hide the failed requests that made the answer possible.</p>
  <p>The awkward documents deserve deliberate tests: two different totals, a revised version, a table continued on another page, a field that is mentioned but not filled in. Use examples appropriate to your work. Fixtures should only be public when they are synthetic or cleared for publication.</p>
  <h2>Where the thresholds sit</h2>
  <p>Usable completions, schema validity and task acceptance each need their own threshold. If a wrong field is especially costly, make that failure a release blocker even when the average looks acceptable. The reviewed examples and the exact schema version stay with the result.</p>
  <p>Your own results log can then say where an extractor is reliable enough for the job and where a person still needs to check it. A green parser alone cannot make that decision.</p>
    <PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
